# Conformance fixtures are byte-exact evidence.
#
# `tools/spec-assertions.json` records offsets measured against Obsidian 1.13.7
# reading these files as LF (for example sub-02 expects a block at 10..48). With
# core.autocrlf=true a Windows checkout rewrites them to CRLF, every offset grows
# by the number of preceding line breaks, and the conformance suite fails on a
# machine where the code is fine. Pin the Markdown fixtures to LF so they mean
# the same thing everywhere.
#
# Only `*.md` is listed. Attachments (pixel.png, min.pdf) and the .obsidian /
# .canvas JSON are byte-exact assets: never mark them `text`, or Git rewrites
# them and the fixture stops matching what the reverse-engineering recorded.
research/fixtures/**/*.md text eol=lf

# The cross-repo MemoryPack fixture is copied verbatim into the Workbench tree
# and compared byte for byte. Keep it LF so a Windows checkout does not rewrite
# it and break that equality.
tools/fixtures/*.json text eol=lf

# The parser is still required to cope with CRLF *content* (notes written on
# Windows outside git); tools/vault.test.mjs covers that with an inline CRLF
# document rather than relying on the checkout.
